  "question": {
    "text": "The function $f(x)=\\tan ^{-1}(\\sin x+\\cos x)$ is an increasing function in",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "LWERS",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "HCssR",
      "text": "\n$\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "hPU4P",
      "text": "\n$\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "JdEEL",
      "text": "\n$\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$f^{\\prime}(x)=\\frac{1}{1+(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2}(\\cos x-\\sin x)$\n$=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\cos \\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}{1+(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2}$\n$f(x)$ is increasing if $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x+\\frac{\\pi}{4} < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n$-\\frac{3 \\pi}{4} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{4}$\nhence $f(x)$ is increasing when $x \\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$.",
    "image": null
